    <h2>1. Krillion.si (Endless Mode) — The Ultimate Reverse-Trivia Sanctuary</h2>
    <p>
      The greatest drawback of most daily puzzles is the dreaded 24-hour waiting barrier. On <strong><a href="/endless" class="text-abyss-cyan underline">Krillion.si</a></strong>, players can enjoy both the official synchronized <strong>Daily Dive</strong> and an unlimited <strong>Endless Sinker Mode</strong>. With hundreds of curated prompts across history, ancient civilizations, oceanic biodiversity, and periodic chemistry, it is the premier arena for testing your factual obscurity.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Pointless (The Spiritual Predecessor)</h2>
    <p>
      Before Krillion digitized the hadal trench scoring curve, the long-running BBC game show <em>Pointless</em> pioneered the reverse-trivia concept: 100 people are surveyed, and contestants must identify correct answers that zero of the surveyed 100 gave. Krillion modernizes this concept into a browser-native deep-sea dive with algorithmic community logs.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Cine2Nerdle — For Film & Obscure Cinema Nerds</h2>
    <p>
      If your favorite category in Krillion is the Academy Awards and cinema prompts, Cine2Nerdle is the definitive peer puzzle. It connects actors, directors, and lesser-known character roles into an interactive grid, heavily punishing players who only know mainstream blockbusters.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Semantle — For Conceptual Semantic Deductions</h2>
    <p>
      While not factual reverse-trivia, Semantle rewards counter-intuitive thought. You guess a secret word using natural language processing similarity scores, forcing you to break away from obvious synonyms.
    </p>

